  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that neomycin interacts with hepatocytes, which are the target cells of the drug, and that these hepatocytes are targeted by a series of compounds, including [(1S,2S,6R,7R,9R,10S,11S,12R,14S)-11-hydroxy-14-methyl-5-methylidene-4-oxospiro[3,13-dioxatetracyclo[8.4.0.02,6.012,14]tetradecane-9,2'-oxirane]-7-yl] (E)-4-hydroxy-2-methylbut-2-enoate, 3-Bromo-4-(2,3-Dibromo-4,5-Dihydroxybenzyl)-5-Methoxymethylpyrocatecol, and 5-[[(2S,3R,4R)-4-(1,3-benzodioxol-5-ylmethyl)-2-methoxyoxolan-3-yl]methyl]-1,3-benzodioxole. All these compounds have a common target of SNU-387, a cell line that is known to be sensitive to these types of molecules. This indicates that neomycin may exert its effects through a mechanism that involves the targeting of hepatocytes, which are sensitive to these compounds. The evidence suggests that neomycin may act by inhibiting the function of these target compounds, potentially through a direct interaction or by modulating cellular pathways that are involved in the activity of these compounds. The presence of multiple pathways that lead to the same target, SNU-387, supports the idea that neomycin may have a broad or specific effect on hepatocytes, and that the observed interactions are consistent with a mechanism of action that involves the targeting of these cells.",
